== The Means of This Grace

Having given you some #emph[directions] for the #emph[manner] of giving your alms, I will briefly show you the means by which you may attain this grace of #emph[Christian charity.]

Now the #emph[means] I will prescribe will be of two kinds:

- 1\. Those that tend to bring your hearts to be willing to do this duty.
- 2\. Those that tend to help you to manage it better.

First, as #emph[means to bring your hearts to be willing to set about this duty, take these.]

#emph[Often call to mind, and print in your#super[+] memories, the many precepts in the Scriptures that require this duty at your hands; and also the many gracious promises God has made there to encourage his people in it.] And then reason with yourself like this: #emph[What? Has God] commanded me #emph[to give to my brother according to his need] (Deut 15:10)? #emph[To cast my bread upon the waters] (Eccl 11:1)? And has our blessed Savior commanded me #emph[to give to the poor] (Luke 6:38)?

And has the Lord, to encourage me in it, promised #emph[that he will bless me in all my works, and prosper me in all that I put my hand to, in my trade] and #emph[calling? That my barns will be filled with plenty? That what we give or lend to the poor, he will pay us back with interest? That though I cast my bread upon the waters,] where it may seem to be lost, #emph[yet I will find it after many days,] and it will certainly be returned into my bosom with increase? And has my #emph[Savior,] to encourage me, promised #emph[that if I give to the poor, it will be given to me again, good measure, pressed down, and shaken together, running over] (Luke 6:38)? Then certainly #emph[there is no shorter way to thrive and prosper in the world than by my liberality to the poor.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Prefer heaven and the things of heaven to this earth and earthly things.] Though the temporal reward I have insisted on so much is a great encouragement, yet the greatest encouragement is the #emph[spiritual] and #emph[eternal] reward the Lord has promised. And so that this encouragement may prevail the more, #emph[learn to esteem and prize things spiritual and eternal above all things that are only temporal.] Suppose it should happen that you#super[+] never receive back #emph[in kind] what you give; yet count those #emph[temporal] things sold at the best price when what you receive for them is #emph[spiritual] and #emph[eternal.] Value #emph[grace] and #emph[glory] above all the #emph[treasures of the earth,] and count yourselves to grow rich men as you abound in grace and in your evidences for glory. And count it an unthrifty saving and holding back when you#super[+] have an opportunity to gain in #emph[your inner man] by spending #emph[your outward substance.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Until heaven is valued above earth, this great argument encouraging acts of bounty and mercy will lose its efficacy. When God says, “Cast your bread upon the waters, for after many days you will find it again,” the worldling will be ready to ask, But how will I find it? In what way will it be returned to me? Will I have #emph[bread] for #emph[bread,] or #emph[money] for my #emph[bread,] or #emph[houses or lands] for my #emph[money?] Very likely you may. But suppose not; yet it will be better than that. You will have #emph[in grace] what you part with in #emph[bread] or #emph[money;] you will reap #emph[in heaven] what you sow #emph[on the waters.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Yes, but the worldling will reply, #emph[I do not mean to part with my bread or my money that way.] No, I know you do not; but the reason is that you love this earth more than heaven. You say, Will #emph[grace] feed me? Will #emph[grace] clothe me? Can I keep myself and my family? Can we live and be maintained, and be kept from poverty and beggary here, by hopes of glory hereafter?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Go, fool, and learn better what God is, and what his everlasting mercy is. Let the lasting treasures, the durable riches, be prized and valued more by you, and this will stop your mouth and silence your heart from such vain reasonings. You will never again say, I cannot spare my bread or my money, when you see that by spending it for God you make him your debtor, who will pay it back to you in spiritual and eternal good things, once you count these better than your bread or your money.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But until you have come to be settled in this judgment and belief, both that the good things of God are infinitely better than the good things of this earth, and that what you sow on earth you will certainly reap in heaven, until you come in good earnest to be of this mind, God’s poor are likely to be little the better for you.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[It is this, beloved, that will effectually do it, that will open your#super[+] hearts and open your hands in bounty and liberality for God: when you come to be rooted in the practical belief of this #emph[principle,] on which the practice of all religion stands and is upheld in the world, #emph[that heaven is better than earth;] and when this other is added to it, #emph[that what is sincerely laid out for God on earth will certainly be repaid in heaven;] besides the return which the Lord often makes to the charitable here in this world.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. For the means that tend to help you manage it better, take these that follow.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Either follow the example of the early saints, in setting apart something every Lord’s day out of the past week’s earnings.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Or, 2. #emph[Devote to God a certain portion of your yearly income for charitable uses.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[That the former way, setting apart something every Lord’s day for the relief of the poor, was the practice of the early Christians is clear from the apostle’s words to the #emph[Corinthians] (1 Cor 16:1, 2): “Now about the collection for the saints, you#super[+] are to do as I directed the churches of Galatia. On the first day of every week (which is the Lord’s day) each of you#super[+] should set aside a portion and save it up, as God has prospered him.” This way and course concerns in particular those who live on their daily labor.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But to gentlemen and others who live on their rents and offices, and to merchants and citizens who cast up their accounts once a year, I would commend the latter way: to devote to God a certain portion of their yearly income, by rent or otherwise, and then, separating it from the rest, to count it #emph[a sacred stock] for the poor, not to be used for any other purpose.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[All men by nature (through the corruption that is in them) are hard-hearted and close-handed, very unwilling to part with anything considerable for charitable uses when an occasion comes suddenly. But when they have set something apart beforehand, their ears, hearts and hands are open to every charitable appeal, and they are very ready and forward to give to it the more liberally out of what is laid by, rejoicing that they have met with so good an object of charity.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[As for the #emph[quota pars,] what proportion every one ought to set apart out of his yearly income for good uses, I do not find it particularly set down in the word of God, and therefore I dare not positively determine it. All the more so because there are so many circumstances that alter the case that no certain rule can be given. I will leave it to the discretion and honest judgment of the prudent Christian.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But though the Scripture does not determine the exact proportion to be set apart for good works, yet it does command us #emph[to give in proportion to what God has given us; to open our hands wide to the needs of the poor; to be rich in good works,] and the like. Yes, the Scripture commends to us the examples of very bountiful Christians: of #emph[Dorcas,] of whom it is said that she “was always occupied with works of kindness and charity” (Acts 9:36); of #emph[Cornelius,] of whom it is said that he “gave generously to the people” (Acts 10:2); and of the #emph[Macedonians,] of whom it is said that “they gave according to their ability and even beyond it” (2 Cor 8:3). It likewise commends to us the example of #emph[Jacob,] who, in testimony of his thankfulness to God for what God would give him, vowed the tenth of it to God for pious and charitable uses, as in Gen 28:20, 22: “Then Jacob made a vow, saying, … ‘Of all that You give me I will surely give You a tenth.’ ”]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[These examples are left on record for us to imitate; for as the apostle says, “everything that was written in the past was written for our instruction” (Rom 15:4), and for our admonition. Most of those I have heard or read of who have taken this course, consecrating to God a certain portion of their estate for charitable uses, have followed the example of #emph[Jacob] in giving a tenth part to God. And as God greatly blessed #emph[Jacob] after his vow, so he has likewise blessed very many others after a like vow to God.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Mr. #emph[Stock,] in his funeral sermon at the burial of #emph[Lord Harrington,] speaking of his charity, declared that in his lifetime he gave the tenth part of his yearly revenue to the poor. Mr. #emph[Gataker,] in his funeral sermon at the burial of Mr. #emph[John Parker,] merchant and citizen of #emph[London,] speaking of his charity, says #emph[that at his first effectual call, among other things he then resolved on, this was one: to set apart every year a tenth of his gain for the relief of the poor and needy; and that from that time God abundantly advanced his estate.] And no doubt, if those #emph[merchants] who usually insure their goods when they fear danger at sea would sincerely promise to God, in testimony of their thankfulness to him, the tenth of what came safe into their hands, they might receive far greater returns than they do.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[In the life of Dr. #emph[Hammond,] written by Dr. #emph[Fell,] I find this statement: “The rate and sum of what the doctor devoted was the tenth of all his income, and in this he was so strictly punctual that commonly the first thing he did was to compute and set apart the poor man’s share.” And how his stock increased, notwithstanding his abundant giving for the relief of others, the history declares at large. I read the like in the life of Mr. #emph[William Wheatly,] minister of #emph[Banbury:] how for many years he set apart the #emph[tenth] of his yearly income, from both his #emph[temporal] and his #emph[ecclesiastical] revenues, and that #emph[his estate prospered the better after he took that course.] To these I could add many more out of the lives of good and charitable men, and out of various funeral sermons which are printed and published. And I could name multitudes of men now living who have devoted a like or greater portion of their estates to charitable uses, and are ready to attest the truth of this discourse, #emph[that they are no losers, but great gainers in temporal things, by what they have given to the poor.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Mr. #emph[Richard Baxter,] in his learned and useful work called #emph[A Christian Directory,] part 4, #emph[fol.] 195, on the #emph[quota pars,] what proportion is fit for most men to devote to charitable uses, and whether the tenth part of their increase is not ordinarily a fit proportion, has this statement:]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Though the proportion of the tenth part is too much for some, and much too little for others, yet for the most part I think it as likely a proportion as it is fit for another to prescribe in particular.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Yet I would not be thought to impose this as a proportion to be kept by all; for not all men’s hearts are equally enlarged with love and bounty. And there are some who have no more than is necessary for the comfortable support of themselves and their family; from them less is required for the relief of others. But I would advise those whom the Lord has blessed with a plentiful estate, so that their necessary expenses are, or might be, far less than their incomes, to devote a #emph[tenth part] of their #emph[whole] yearly income to charitable uses. I say, of their #emph[whole yearly income,] without first deducting any part of it for food, clothes, or other necessary expenses; for the tenth of the remainder would be a very small proportion for a rich man to devote to charity, and far short of what the Scripture seems to call for.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And know for certain that this course is so far from being a means of diminishing your stock and store that, if it is managed as it ought to be, according to the rules and directions given before, it is the surest and safest means, the shortest course, of increasing it, through the secret blessing of God which goes with it. This has been sufficiently shown in the foregoing discourse.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Be careful on whom you bestow your alms.] For by giving to common beggars who are able to work, and yet are so lazy that they will not work but would live by the sweat of other men’s brows, I say, by relieving such, we will both maintain them in their idle and wicked life, and neglect those who are truly poor and ought to be looked on as the proper objects of our charity, or at least cut them short of what rightly belongs to them.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I am not against relieving all beggars. Some of them, I know, are blind, others lame, aged, and past their work. These #emph[impotent] poor, in view of their present condition, are objects of charity. But not the #emph[impudent] poor, who have strength enough to work and will not, those #emph[canting vagrants] who are the burden of the earth and the shame of the kingdom: for these I have no charity. Neither had the apostle St. #emph[Paul,] who was full of compassion toward God’s poor; but for #emph[the devil’s poor] he gave this command, “If anyone is unwilling to work, he shall not eat” (2 Thess 3:10), so that hunger and need might drive them to labor. #emph[Erasmus] mentions a kind of mercy most fit for such: #emph[it is misericordia puniens] \[a mercy that punishes\]. Surely the #emph[whip] is more their due than #emph[food,] and #emph[Bridewell] [a house of correction] to receive them rather than an #emph[almshouse.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[III.] Another #emph[means] for attaining this grace of Christian charity is this: #emph[Be earnest with God in prayer, that he would be pleased to give you that adorning and enriching grace of charity, and accept your offering.] This sacrifice, as it must be offered up to heaven, so it must be fetched from heaven. The gift of a merciful and charitable heart is one of those gifts that come down from above, from the Father of lights (Jas 1:17). If you do not receive this gift from God, your poor brother is likely to have no gift from you. Now it is your prayer that must fetch down this gift from God. Lift up your heart to the Lord, and beg for a merciful and compassionate soul. Beg God to make you ready for every good work, willing to share with the poor members of Christ according to your ability and their need, so that by this you may lay up in store for yourself a good foundation for the time to come, and so take hold of eternal life. Pray for a willing heart, and pray that your gift may be accepted, that your alms may be such a sacrifice, and so offered up, that the Lord may be well pleased with it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Having thus shown you the #emph[means] to be performed on our part for attaining and exercising this grace of charity, I will close this discourse by answering two or three objections.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Object.] 1. Some object the greatness of their charge, the number of their children, who must be provided for, and say that they therefore have little to give to the poor.]

Answ. 1. #emph[Do you have many children? Then you ought all the more to be charitable, so that by this you may entail God’s blessing on them, which is the best portion and surest inheritance you can leave them.] That will secure their stock and improve their store; whereas unmercifulness to the poor will entail a #emph[curse] instead of a #emph[blessing] on the persons and estates of your children.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. You who plead the number of your children as an excuse for your stinginess: how justly may God then lessen the number, and so take away this excuse by taking your children from you!]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. #emph[Suppose you have many children, perhaps ten; count God as one more, and give him a child’s portion.] I mean, give the poor, for his sake, as much every year as one child costs you. Give them a child’s portion, and I dare boldly say you will not be the poorer, but rather a great gainer. For by this you will procure God’s blessing on yourself and your children, which in truth is all in all; and without it, all is likely to come to nothing, as we see by daily experience. This made St. #emph[Augustine] say, #emph[Haeredi praestabis, quicquid misericordiae causa de ejus portion detraxeris,] Aug. De Tempore, Serm. 76. What you give out of your estate to charitable uses will be no loss to your children, but rather a great advantage. Yes, the Spirit of God, in setting forth the happiness of a merciful man, says that his children will fare the better for his goodness and bounty, as in Ps 37:26: “He is ever generous and quick to lend, and his children are blessed”; that is, God will bless his children, not only with spiritual but also with temporal blessings.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Obj.] Many who profess religion in these days are very barren in good works.]

Answ. #emph[Fruitless and uncharitable Christians may make a profession of religion, but without question they are not sound Christians, nor sincere in their profession.] For it is most certain that #emph[the elect of God put on hearts of mercy and compassion,] as the apostle says (Col 3:12). And therefore an #emph[uncharitable Christian] is a plain contradiction. For who is a Christian but he who conforms himself to the law and life of Christ? The substance of Christ’s law is #emph[charity,] and his life was spent in going about doing good, one way or another, to the souls and bodies of men and women.

Therefore whoever has this world’s goods, and sees his brother in need, and shuts up his heart of compassion from him, and will not share anything considerable toward his needs, let his profession be ever so fair and glorious in the eyes of men, yet certainly he is only a rotten professor of religion and a dissembling hypocrite in the sight of God. In Matthew 25:34-36 we find that liberality to the members of Christ is made the special touchstone of true faith and godliness. For the charitable are those who at the great day of judgment will appear as the #emph[sheep] at Christ’s right hand, and will hear that joyful sentence of absolution: “Come, you#super[+] who are blessed by My Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world. For I was hungry and you#super[+] gave Me something to eat, I was thirsty and you gave Me something to drink, I was a stranger and you took Me in, I was naked and you clothed Me, I was sick and you looked after Me, I was in prison and you visited Me.”

And on the other side, all unmerciful men who have not relieved Christ in his members, whatever profession of religion they have made here in this world, will yet appear at the last day as the #emph[goats] set at Christ’s left hand, and will hear that dreadful sentence of condemnation: “Depart from Me, you#super[+] who are cursed, into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels. For I was hungry and you#super[+] gave Me nothing to eat, I was thirsty and you gave Me nothing to drink, I was a stranger and you did not take Me in, naked and you did not clothe Me, sick and in prison and you did not visit Me.” Hence it appears that, as mercy and bounty to the poor members of Christ is a conspicuous evidence of true faith and sincere godliness in a Christian, and clearly declares him godly, so illiberality and unmercifulness to them is a clear mark of unbelief and ungodliness, and clearly declares him to be an unbeliever, and so a wicked and profane person. Therefore I would much question the sincerity of the faith and piety of those who make a profession of religion and yet show little or no charity to the poor members of Christ. For as the apostle #emph[James] says (Jas 1:27), “Pure and undefiled religion before our God and Father is this: to care for orphans and widows in their distress.” This implies that works of mercy and charity rightly performed are clear evidences of the truth of our religion and the sincerity of our profession. And if you#super[+] look into the Scriptures, you will scarcely read of a man truly godly without reading also that he was merciful and charitable, forward in every good work.

So much will suffice for this point of doctrine.
